Build one compact horizontal row per column of a phyloseq's \code{tax_table}
(via \code{\link[tidypq:tax_table_to_df]{tidypq::tax_table_to_df()}}), stacked into a single tight figure so
many columns can be scanned at a glance. Factor/character/logical columns
become a single stacked bar spanning the full row: each category is a
colored zone sized by its proportion of the total, \code{NA} is a dark grey
zone, and categories that individually represent less than \code{threshold} of
the total are drawn in grey with a stripe motif (via \code{ggpattern}, when
installed) and left unlabeled (only zones at or above \code{threshold} get a
small text label). A column is treated as boolean-like as soon as one of
its values reads \code{"true"} or \code{"false"} (case-insensitive, so logical
columns and mixes like \code{c(TRUE, FALSE, "uncertain")} both qualify): the
color scheme is then fixed across every such column (olive green for
true, brick red for false, a blue gradient for any other value), instead
of the arbitrary categorical palette used for other factor columns.
The last such bar row carries a shared \code{"Proportion"} x-axis (ticks at
0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1) for the whole group; other bar rows have theirs
hidden to avoid repeating it. Numeric columns become a thin horizontal
raincloud (violin + boxplot + jittered points) with the number of
non-\code{NA} values annotated in the top-right corner (and the proportion
of \code{NA} values too, when there is at least one \code{NA}).
}
